\begin{abstract}
\noindent
We want to know how artificial intelligence is changing which companies get
founded, where, and in what industries. The obstacle is that almost everything
we know about startups comes from a few commercial company databases, and those
databases do not record firms at random. This paper explains, in plain terms,
how we built a dataset of $988{,}576$ firms from six kinds of source, how we
decided which of them count as ``AI,'' and what we checked before believing our
own numbers. Three results follow. AI's share of newly founded firms rises from
$3.0\%$ in 2000 to $59.0\%$ in 2025. The firms the commercial databases miss are
about twice as likely to be AI-related. And a language model asked to guess
missing company details will readily invent them---which is why we forbade it
from doing so.
\end{abstract}

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} artificial intelligence, entrepreneurship,
measurement, coverage bias

% ============================================================================
\section{The question, and why it is hard}
% ============================================================================

Artificial intelligence is widely expected to change who starts companies and
what those companies do \citep{fossen2024ai}. There is early evidence that
generative AI raises productivity for existing workers
\citep{brynjolfsson2023genai} and that it is already shifting entry decisions
\citep{bena2025prompted}. To study this at the level of the firm, we need to
observe firms.

That is the problem. Research on entrepreneurship almost always runs on
commercial startup databases, and those databases are assembled by companies
with commercial priorities. A firm enters them when someone has a reason to
record it: it raised money, it was covered by the press, or it registered
itself. \citet{dalle2017crunchbase} document this for one widely used database
and warn that coverage is uneven in ways that matter for research conclusions.

So a rise in ``AI firms founded'' can mean two very different things:

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.4em, itemsep=2pt]
  \item more AI firms are actually being founded, or
  \item the databases got better at noticing them.
\end{enumerate}

\noindent
Our approach is to collect firms from sources with \emph{different} reasons for
recording a company---funders, code hosts, accelerator pages---so that we can
compare what the commercial databases hold against firms they never picked up.
The gap between the two is itself a measurement.

