We report the yield plainly because it is unflattering. Of $410$ sites
attempted, $220$ ($53.7\%$) ever produced a successful extraction. Yield is very
skewed: a successful site returns $22.9$ records on average, but the median is
$6$ and the maximum is $814$. Estimating the total from the average overstates
it roughly fourfold. We made exactly this mistake in our own planning before
auditing the sites one by one.

\subsection{Merging records that name the same firm}

The same company arrives from several sources under slightly different names.
Deciding when two records are one firm is record linkage, a problem with a long
methodological literature \citep{abramitzky2021linking}.

Our production rule is deliberately conservative. Each record gets one key: its
web domain, reduced to the registrable part, if it has one; otherwise a
normalised version of its name. Two records are the same firm when the keys are
equal. A blocklist stops code hosts, documentation sites, and social platforms
from being treated as a company's own domain, which would otherwise merge
unrelated firms that share a hosting provider.

We found one defect worth stating plainly, because it cannot be fixed by tuning.
The name normaliser strips common corporate tokens---and it strips ``AI'' along
with them. For this dataset that is precisely the token separating a new AI firm
from an older company of the same name. No matching threshold can repair a
normaliser that deletes the distinguishing word before matching begins.

\subsection{Deciding whether a firm is ``AI''}

A firm is labelled AI if \emph{any} of four signals fires:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:ai}
A_i \;=\; \mathbb{1}\big[\,
\underbrace{t_i}_{\text{vendor tag}} \;\vee\;
\underbrace{a_i \ge 0.5}_{\text{keyword score}} \;\vee\;
\underbrace{m_i}_{\text{AI mentioned in text}} \;\vee\;
\underbrace{\ell_i}_{\text{model verdict}}
\,\big].
\end{equation}

The signals are ordered cheapest-first, so the expensive one runs only on
records the cheap ones cannot settle. Using a language model for this kind of
classification is now standard practice in economics \citep{dell2025deep}; the
caution we add is about \emph{where} the signals exist, not about whether the
model is accurate.

Two properties of \eqref{eq:ai} shape everything downstream.

\textbf{The signals are not equally available.} Vendor tags cover $7.0\%$ of
database-A firms, $0.7\%$ of database-B firms, and $0.0\%$ of firms found
outside both. Model verdicts were run on $98.8\%$ of database B but only
$2.3\%$ of the unlisted layer. Only the two description-derived signals are
computed identically for every firm. Any comparison across populations must
therefore either accept that asymmetry or restrict to the signals all firms
share. We report both.

\textbf{The keyword score does not transfer across sources.} Database-B records
carry no topic tags and short descriptions, so the highest score they can reach
is $0.3$---below the $0.5$ threshold by construction. Under a tag-or-score
definition database B looks $1.3\%$ AI; under the full union it is $10.7\%$. A
threshold calibrated on one source had silently excluded $281{,}395$ firms in
another. We only noticed by checking the score's attainable maximum per source,
which we recommend as routine practice.

\subsection{Filling in missing fields}

Many firms are missing a founding year, a country, or an industry. We fill these
under a strict rule about \emph{where a value may come from}:

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2.2em, itemsep=2pt]
  \item[$T_1$] an official registry (free, checkable);
  \item[$T_2$] a document we fetched ourselves;
  \item[$T_3$] a language model reading a text we already hold;
  \item[$T_4$] a language model recalling a fact from its own memory ---
        \textbf{prohibited}.
\end{enumerate}

Every stored value keeps its tier and its source alongside it, so any analysis
can filter by origin. Section~\ref{sec:checks} explains what happened when we
briefly allowed $T_4$, and why recording provenance is what let us undo it.

% ============================================================================
\section{What we find}
% ============================================================================

\paragraph{AI's share of new firms rises; the observed count does not.}
Among firms founded in a given year, the AI share climbs from $3.0\%$ in 2000
to $18.9\%$ in 2021 and $59.0\%$ in 2025. The rise survives holding the
selection rule fixed. But the raw \emph{number} of AI firms we observe peaks in
2018 and falls afterwards. That fall is not a decline in entrepreneurship; it is
that recent founding cohorts are thinly indexed---databases take years to catch
up with young firms. Reporting the count without the share would have produced a
headline the data cannot support.

\paragraph{The firms the databases miss are about twice as AI-heavy.}
Firms discovered outside the two commercial databases are $23.8\%$ AI, against
$12.4\%$ and $10.7\%$ for the two databases. This is the central finding, and
the one we tried hardest to break (Section~\ref{sec:checks}).

\paragraph{Geographic concentration reverses, and mostly should not be
believed.} The United States rises from $37\%$ of observed AI firms in the 2017
founding cohort to $85\%$ in the 2025 cohort. We show most of that reversal is
coverage rather than economics: recent non-U.S.\ firms are simply indexed later.
We report it as a caution, not as a finding.

% ============================================================================
\section{What we checked}
\label{sec:checks}
% ============================================================================

A result this convenient to our own argument deserves adversarial testing, so we
attacked the coverage gap four ways. It survives (i) five different definitions
of the AI label; (ii) dropping any single discovery channel; (iii) an
independently adjudicated label on a subsample, where the gap is $19.0\%$ versus
$9.5\%$ ($p = 0.007$); and (iv) a correction for the fact that our classifier is
imperfect, and imperfect in different ways across buckets. For (iv) we use the
standard adjustment for prevalence estimated from a screening test of known
sensitivity and specificity \citep{rogan1978prevalence}. Proportions are
reported with Wilson intervals \citep{wilson1927interval} and differences
between proportions with the method recommended by \citet{newcombe1998intervals}.

The most useful negative result concerns the language model. When we asked it to
\emph{estimate} unstated company attributes, it filled $88\%$ of fields---and
filled them from its own prior, predicting San Francisco at roughly seven times
its true rate in the data. When instructed to answer only from text we supplied,
the fill rate dropped to $2\%$. The model was not malfunctioning; it was doing
what it had been asked. The lesson is that the instruction, not the model, is
the research design decision. Because every value carried its provenance tag,
the contaminated fields could be identified and removed rather than silently
entering the results.

% ============================================================================
\section{What this dataset cannot show}
% ============================================================================

We are explicit about the limits, because the coverage argument cuts both ways.

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.4em, itemsep=3pt]
  \item \textbf{Our own coverage is not random either.} We find firms that leave
        a public trace---a repository, an award, a portfolio page. Firms with no
        online footprint are missing from our data exactly as they are missing
        from the commercial ones.
  \item \textbf{Metadata is thin outside the commercial databases.} Founding
        year is present for $98.8\%$ and $100.0\%$ of the two commercial
        populations, but for only $8.0\%$ of the unlisted layer (rising to
        $29.6\%$ with proxies). Country is present for $48.4\%$. This is why the
        time, geography, and industry analyses run on the commercial population,
        while the unlisted layer is used for the coverage comparison, which needs
        only the AI label.
  \item \textbf{``AI firm'' is a label, not a fact.} It is a decision rule
        applied to noisy text. We report what the rule does rather than claiming
        it identifies a natural category.
  \item \textbf{Recent years are incomplete.} Any statement about the last two or
        three founding cohorts is a statement about indexing speed as much as
        about entrepreneurship.
\end{enumerate}

% ============================================================================
\section{Conclusion}
% ============================================================================

The substantive finding is that AI entrepreneurship is growing quickly and that
the firms conventional databases miss are disproportionately AI-related---so
studies built only on those databases will understate AI entry.

The methodological finding is smaller but more portable. Three of the errors we
document---a threshold that could not be reached in one source, a normaliser
that deleted the token carrying the signal, and a model that answered from
memory when asked to estimate---were invisible in aggregate output and appeared
only when we checked a mechanism directly. None would have shown up in a
robustness table. We report them because the same three are easy to reproduce in
any project that assembles firm data from heterogeneous sources with model
assistance.

